# SlipCurve code corpus — Drive mirror

This folder mirrors the text artefacts of `corpus/` in the SlipCurve repository.
It is a **reading copy**. The repository is the source of truth, and the build
gates run against the bytes there, not against these files.

## What is here

| file | what it is |
|---|---|
| `Cited clauses — verbatim` | every paragraph and table figure the clearance engine relies on, lifted verbatim from the source XML |
| `NOTICE — 29 CFR 1910 Subpart S` | rights, provenance, retrieval date, source SHA-256 |
| `NOTICE — 29 CFR 1926` | rights, provenance, retrieval date, source SHA-256 |
| `corpus directory README` | why `corpus/` exists, how it differs from `vendor/` and `codes/`, and how a document is added |

## What is NOT here, and why

The source XML itself is not mirrored. It is 3.5 MB and 286 KB respectively, it
already lives in the repository at `corpus/<id>/source.xml.gz`, and it is
re-fetchable at any time from the URL recorded in each NOTICE. Keeping a third
copy in a place no gate checks would create a document that can drift from the
one the build actually verifies against — which is precisely the failure the
SHA-256 records exist to prevent.

To re-fetch either original:

    curl -o osha-1926.xml \
      "https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-08-01/title-29.xml?chapter=XVII&subchapter=R&part=1926"

    curl -o osha-1910S.xml \
      "https://www.ecfr.gov/api/versioner/v1/full/2026-08-01/title-29.xml?part=1910&subpart=S"

Then check it against the SHA-256 in the matching NOTICE.

